This is not ever going to show up in any exam, but is still a nice exercise for
pseudocode -> python translation.

You do not need to understand this algorithm at all, you just need to translate it into
Python.

Here are some functions you might need to copy paste:

def right_rotate(x, n):
    # rotate right on 32 bits
    return ((x >> n) | (x << (32 - n))) & 0xFFFFFFFF

def to_bytes(message):
    # convert string to list of byte values
    return list(message.encode())

def to_32bit(x):
    return x & 0xFFFFFFFF

And here are the python equivalents for these bitwise operators:

MOD - %
AND - &
OR - |
NOT - ~
XOR - ^
>> (left shift) - >>
<< (right shift) - <<

To generate an empty list of 64:
[0 for i in range(64)]
